---
import config from '../config.json';
import { PLATFORMS } from '../lib/rsvp.mjs';
import { newTabQualifier } from '../lib/links.mjs';

// The band is the site's EXIT, so it answers the three things a visitor asks at the bottom of a page:
// where do I go next, who is this, and when was this written. What used to sit here was a slogan
// ("Join our Math ∑ Book Community") with a glyph pulsing at 0.5s forever and no links at all — the
// glyph was the second-largest thing on the screen (29.9px), which is exactly what D112 forbids: the
// display step belongs to a screen's locator, never to a punchline. Operator ruling, 2026-11-02: the
// footer is designed entirely, and the pulse goes.
//
// Why there is no button in here: the primary control is an `ink` FILL (DESIGN.md "Buttons"), and an
// ink fill on the ink band is invisible. So paths on the band are named by hue instead — the
// `--color-path-*-on-ink` steps exist for precisely this ("the same four paths on the dark band" — the four
// are the outbound platform steps, calendar / Meetup / Discord / Mobilizon, and an internal link like
// `/chat` or `/schedule` carries no hue at all, it is the band's own text colour; the hue is what marks a
// path that leaves the site, 5.15 meetup / 5.41 discord @measured 2026-11-14 by `npm run check:contrast`) — and the band
// gets exactly one dark region, which is what `check:render` counts.
//
// No "Home" link: the serif wordmark below is the home path, same as in the nav (D100, and the operator
// confirmed the fourth nav link goes too).
//
// The two outbound platform anchors below carry `data-rsvp-platform` handles since 2026-11-15 (round-4
// §10's ceiling on outbound links outside the band). That attribute is what lets
// `scripts/check-rsvp-page.mjs` prove the band and the schedule's standing row are one map rather than two
// copies of it: `src/lib/rsvp.mjs`'s `EXIT_BAND_PLATFORMS` names the club-wide exits the band owns, and a
// standing row that repeated one of them is now a defect the gate reads off the built bytes instead of a
// page with the same URL painted twice.
const year = new Date().getFullYear();
---

<footer class="bg-ink text-band-text">
  {/* `py-12` (48px), not `py-10` (40px): DESIGN.md rules section air as ONE step at every width — 48px on
     every band — and this was the last off-ladder spacing write left in the band. Caught by
     `npm run check:scale` (S042, D321), the detector DESIGN.md's own Enforcement section says
     `check:classes` cannot be: Tailwind v4 resolves `py-10`, so "the class exists" was never
     "the value is on the scale". The band gains 8px top and bottom. */}
  <div class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
    {/* The read's §1 asks for the footer as "complete map, this order and nothing else" and names one
        thing it forbids: a "Where to next" heading. The visible micro-label here WAS that orphan — a
        caption over a `<nav>` that already carries its own name (`aria-label="Where to next"`), the same
        class as the eyebrow D309 deleted above every page's first heading (DESIGN.md, "Nothing sits above
        a page's first heading"). Deleting it is D335: the band still answers the three questions its own
        comment names, and the label was the site calling its own map a question. The `aria-label` stays —
        it is invisible to eyes and is what keeps axe's `region` rule off this element — and the band's
        air is now its own `py-12` (DESIGN.md, "Section air is one step at every width"), so the
        `mt-1` that used to sit under the label goes with it. */}
    <!-- Targets are 32px tall (16px line box + py-2), the same accepted shape as the nav links; do not
         shave the pad. `outline-band-text` is the focus ring HERE: accent on ink measures 2.97, which the
         oracle bans, and `ratify-check` fails this file if an accent ring comes back. -->
    {/* MS41 (S090, D467): the three exits in this nav are the site's standing asks, so the nav and the feed's own
        sentence together DECLARE themselves the context for those three actions (`data-cta-block`, one element for
        three destinations because they are one map). Before this the band printed a bare platform name - "Discord" -
        on all six routes, and the 64 words nearest it were the colophon's trademark boilerplate: nothing said what
        the click does @measured 2026-11-21 by `node design/cta-inventory.mjs`. The two labels now carry the noun
        the invite actually implies (a **group**, a **server**), which is the disclosure the research bought from
        Discord's own Invite Object ("a code that when used, adds a user to a guild" @retrieved 2026-11-20 from
        https://discord.com/developers/docs/resources/invite), and the words are the site's own existing idiom -
        `signUpPaths()` already prints "Join the Meetup group", and `check:join-page` already pins "Join the Meetup
        group" on `/join`. `check:rsvp-page` and `check:page-exit` read the `data-rsvp-platform` handles and the
        hrefs, not these words, so relabelling moves no gate. */}
    <div data-cta-block="calendar-feed meetup-group discord-server">
    <nav
      aria-label="Where to next"
      class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-2"
    >
      {/* Round-6 review (2026-11-16), `/dates`/Remove: "the footer nav that repeats the header nav verbatim"
          (D395). Dates, Chat and Ways in are deleted from the band rather than reworded: every page the build
          emits carries those three paths in its own nav at the top, so the band printed the same three hrefs a
          second time on every route - 3 anchors × 5 routes - while the body of the page already offers them.
          What the band keeps is what only the band carries: the feed, and the two platform exits. The pair of
          rules that makes this safe lives in `scripts/check-page-exit.mjs`: the band still needs two paths out,
          and a new rule `exit-band-nav-repeat` fails a band that starts repeating the header nav again.
          D309's band-name-drift rule in `check:naming` is untouched; with these links gone it is simply never
          asked. The band's identity line (`href="/"`) is not part of this nav, so the page still links home. */}
      <a
        href='/events.ics'
        data-cta="calendar-feed"
        class='py-2 text-label font-medium hover:text-accent-on-ink hover:underline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-band-text'
      >
        Add to a calendar
      </a>
      <!-- Round-4 brief §9b (2026-11-15, D346): this href used to be `${config.URLs.meetupGroup}/join` - still
           readable at `git show 5cd128f:src/components/Footer.astro:81` - so the site addressed one group two ways
           from the band every page ends with. §9b asks for one survivor and the other deleted; the survivor is the
           **config-declared root**, because it is the string `URLs.meetupGroup` holds, the shape `MEETUP_GROUP_URL_SHAPE`
           (src/lib/rsvp.mjs:76) requires of it, and the only Meetup group URL carrying a dated `_verified` stamp
           (`status: 200, verifiedAt: 2026-11-10` in src/config.json). Measured off this commit's artifact @measured
           2026-11-15 by `grep -o minneapolis-mathematics-book-club/join dist/client/* | wc -l`: the `/join` form now
           paints **0 times on all five built pages**, and the root paints 2 on `/`, 1 on `/chat`, 2 on `/join`, 1 on
           `/schedule` (besides 5 per-event pages), 2 on `/404`. The `/join` form survives only inside `joinUrl()`
           (src/lib/rsvp.mjs:127, called at :416) - the resolver's named "no page yet for this date" state, which is a
           per-date action with its own stamp that `check:rsvp` reads - so nothing here is dead code, and no unprobed
           URL is a primary control. -->
      <a
        href={config.URLs.meetupGroup}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        data-cta="meetup-group"
        data-rsvp-platform={PLATFORMS.meetup}
        class='py-2 text-label font-medium text-path-meetup-on-ink hover:underline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-band-text'
        >
        Join the Meetup group<span class="sr-only">{newTabQualifier('Join the Meetup group', PLATFORMS.meetup)}</span>
      </a>
      <a
        href={config.URLs.discordInvite}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        data-cta="discord-server"
        data-rsvp-platform={PLATFORMS.discord}
        class='py-2 text-label font-medium text-path-discord-on-ink hover:underline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-band-text'
        >
        Join the Discord server<span class="sr-only">{newTabQualifier('Join the Discord server', PLATFORMS.discord)}</span>
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- The feed's own sentence, in the band beside the path it describes (round-5 communication read A5,
        2026-11-16, D369). q21 — "am I told when a date moves?" — was answered on `/schedule` and nowhere else,
        because the clause lived once in `src/components/SignUpLinks.astro`, under that page's own calendar
        link. The band paints the same offer (`Add to a calendar`, `href='/events.ics'`) on all five emitted
        pages, so every other page handed the reader the feed link with nothing to qualify it — they subscribe
        to a file and are never told it moves with the club. Echoed here, **not linked**: the band's existing
        path is the way in, so no second `/events.ics` anchor and no new URL (R1: do not add URLs to a page
        for a gate). The band stays one string on all five routes — `node design/communication-price.mjs`
        before this clause: 6,983 raw octets a page, deleting the whole band 2,269 · 2,332 · 2,342 · 2,303 ·
        2,296 gzip on /, /404, /chat, /join, /schedule = 11,542 across five pages. Against this commit's
        artifact the same probe prints the grown band; `node design/communication-census.mjs` clusters the
        echo with `/schedule`'s standing sentence, and `design/copy-duplicates.json` keeps that pair as
        `status: deliberate` — the echo is the point, since the fact has to travel with every place the file
        is offered. -->
    <p class="mt-2 text-micro max-w-measure">
      Add to a calendar: Google, Apple and Outlook can all follow it, and it changes when the club changes a date.
    </p>
    </div>

    {/* Identity, not a locator: 16px serif at every width, the same face and size as the nav wordmark,
         because the club's name is carried by the face (D95/D100), and the identity is the WORD alone.
         This is a server-side comment, not an HTML one: an HTML comment in a component ships to every
         page (measured here — an HTML comment of this length added 384 characters to all six routes),
         and the prose below is for the reader of the source, never for the visitor. The summand glyph
         went on 2026-11-16 (S050/D379): the operator's word on it is that it was a fun unique thing,
         out of place on a page this round has now designed — a glyph no route explains is the site
         claiming something about itself that it cannot ground, the same rule D112 already paid for the
         display step (a locator's step, never a punchline). Nothing here swaps the accent, changes the
         size, or adds another glyph; the identity line stays the home path (D100). */}
    <p class="mt-8 font-body text-body font-bold">
      <a
        href='/'
        aria-label="Matheapolis, home"
        class='py-1 hover:underline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-band-text'
      >
        Matheapolis
      </a>
    </p>

    <!-- Small print carries the place and the date, and nothing else: no frequency claim (D92), and the
         old "All rights reserved" boilerplate is gone with the rest of the old band. -->
    <p class="mt-6 text-micro">Minneapolis, Minnesota</p>
    <!-- The old name, in the colophon rather than spliced into the middle of a paragraph about agendas
        (round-7 review, Home/Revise "Formerly the Minneapolis Mathematics Book Club": "if the former name
        matters for search, it belongs in the page title or a footer line, not spliced into the middle of a
        sentence about agendas"). The wording stays the Meetup listing's own name — "Minneapolis Mathematics
        Book Club", not the site's old wordmark — because that is the name six years of members, printed
        flyers and the Meetup group URL actually carry, and it is the string a returning member or an old
        bookmark searches. It moved out of the hero lede because that paragraph is about what a meeting is;
        the colophon is where a page says who it is. The page title keeps D396's shape (the letter offered
        the title as the alternative; the city is what search needs there, and a former name in the title
        would make six titles a subscriber cannot scan). Cost, measured before this move: one extra line box
        in the band at every width, and +28 to +61px of page @measured 2026-11-07 by `npm run round16` when
        the same sentence sat in the lede — the band pays the box once per page, and `check:render`'s band
        section reports whether it still reads as one dark region. -->
    <p class="mt-1 text-micro">Formerly the Minneapolis Mathematics Book Club.</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-micro">&#169; {year} Matheapolis</p>

    <!-- One static line, not a component (round 10 ruling on third-party marks). The site names Meetup and
         Discord in its own type and links out; it no longer paints either company's mark — the Meetup png
         that used to sit in the join card was the most saturated colour on the landing page (0.523% of pixels
         at C 0.226) and it was not ours. Naming a platform is nominative use; borrowing its colour and shape
         to do the naming is what gets a site into an argument. If a mark ever comes back it is the official
         file, unmodified, in clearspace, next to the name it stands for (design/records/0013).

         The line is the whole legal sentence, in the colophon (round-6 review, 2026-11-16, D394, superseding
         D366's split). D366 had put a short clause here and the full pair on `/join`; the reviewer's
         complaint was a legal line sitting in the body of the page a visitor has just decided to come to, which
         is the peak-end rule the same round applied to the room sentence — the last thing read was the coldest
         thing said. What ships now is one sentence, in the band, and the band is the only place it prints:
         it appears on all six emitted pages @measured 2026-11-17 by counting the sentence across `dist/client`,
         and the wording change costs 71 raw octets a page (121 against D366's 50), 426 across the artifact —
         the same probe before this round's copy changes, and `git grep "not sponsored" HEAD -- src` returns
         nothing, which is what says the `/join` half of D366's split had already left the tree. -->
    <p class="mt-1 text-micro max-w-measure">
      Meetup and Discord are the platforms’ own names; this site is the club’s, and is not sponsored or
      endorsed by either.
    </p>
  </div>
</footer>
